;;; class-TypeError.slatl — Motoi Scheme Reference · error-cards
;;; Census key: class:TypeError   ·   family: leaky-host   ·   counted-real
;;; The OTHER leaky-host outcome: a raw JS TypeError whose message does NOT say
;;; "not a function", so it does NOT collide into not-a-procedure — it lands
;;; under its raw class name instead. Surfaced by the mint script from the live
;;; census (observed 2026-07-27), then hand-authored.

(dialect :applies ("motoi") :note "error-card — class:TypeError (leaky host TypeError, non-colliding)")

(error-card :key "class:TypeError"
  :name "Host TypeError (leaked, non-colliding)"
  :family "leaky-host"
  :census-status "counted-real"
  :library "error-cards"
  :training-eligible #t
  :confidentiality "public"
  :provenance "scheme/reference/error-cards/class-TypeError.slatl"
  :status "real"

  :scheme-classification
"Not a Scheme category — a HOST leak. Motoi runs on JavaScript; a few verbs pass
their argument straight into a JS builtin (a `for...of`, a `.map`, a spread)
without a Scheme-level type check first. When the argument is the wrong shape,
the JS engine throws a native `TypeError` and that error escapes the Motoi guard
with its host wording intact. R7RS would have signalled a clean type error at the
verb; here the host's own error is what you see."

  :trigger-code "(for-each display 5)"
  :observed-output "error: lists[0] is not iterable"
  :normalizes-to "class:TypeError"

  :meaning
"This is the SIBLING of not-a-procedure, and the difference between them is the
whole lesson. `normalizeError` (lib/system/verb-stats.js) tries fourteen family
regexes first; one of them is `\\bnot a (procedure|function)\\b`. A leaked
TypeError whose message happens to contain 'is not a function' (e.g. `(reverse 5)`
=> `a.slice is not a function`) MATCHES that regex and is filed under
not-a-procedure — a collision (see not-a-procedure). But a leaked TypeError whose
message reads differently — `lists[0] is not iterable`, `Cannot read properties
of undefined` — matches NO family, has no `namespace/name:` verb prefix, and so
falls through to the class-name bucket: `class:<err.name>` = `class:TypeError`.

So the same underlying cause (a host builtin handed the wrong type) splits across
TWO census keys purely on the wording of the JS message. `not-a-procedure` gets
the '.map/.slice/.find is not a function' leaks; `class:TypeError` gets the 'not
iterable' / 'cannot read properties' leaks. Neither key is a real Scheme
diagnosis — both are the host talking. Seeing `class:TypeError` rise means a verb
is missing a precondition check and letting the runtime speak in its place."

  :common-causes (
    "passing a non-list where a list is iterated — `(for-each f 5)`,
     `(for-each f #f)` — the JS `for...of` throws 'is not iterable'"
    "handing a verb a value whose host representation lacks the method the impl
     reaches for, with a message that does NOT contain 'not a function'"
    "a verb impl that indexes or spreads a host value (`x[0]`, `...x`) before
     checking it is the Scheme type it expects")

  :at-the-verb
"The message names a HOST symbol, not a Scheme verb — `lists[0]`, `a`, `x` are
internal variable names inside the verb's JavaScript, so they will not tell you
which Scheme verb you called. Your locator is the FORM you wrote, not the message:
the verb at the head of the offending expression is the one missing a guard. Read
`lists[0] is not iterable` as 'the verb tried to iterate my second argument and it
was not a list'."

  :how-to-detect
"It escapes at the eval boundary like any thrown error, so `guard` DOES catch it
(unlike fuel-exhausted). Catch it and read the host message as data:"
  :how-to-detect-code
"(display (guard (e (#t (list 'caught (error-object-message e))))
  (for-each display 5)))"
  :how-to-detect-output "(caught lists[0] is not iterable)"
  :how-to-detect-note
"Better than catching the host's words is never provoking them: check the type
yourself and raise a legible Scheme error instead. This turns an opaque
`class:TypeError` into a clear message that says which verb and which argument:"
  :how-to-detect-code-2
"(define (safe-for-each f xs)
  (if (list? xs) (for-each f xs) (error \"for-each: not a list\" xs)))
(display (guard (e (#t (error-object-message e))) (safe-for-each display 5)))"
  :how-to-detect-output-2 "for-each: not a list"

  :fix-shape
"For CODE: guard the argument's type before the verb runs (`list?`, `vector?`,
`string?`), and raise your own `(error \"verb: what\" irritant)` — a Scheme
message with a verb name routes better AND reads better than the host's leak. For
the LANGUAGE: add the precondition check inside the verb impl so it throws a clean
Motoi error (ideally with a `namespace/name:` prefix so it routes to a per-verb
census key) instead of letting the JS builtin throw. That is the same promotion
path misc-uncategorized describes — move a leak out of the class-name dust bin and
into a named coordinate."

  :lesson
"A host language underneath a Scheme leaks its own errors when a verb forgets to
check types first, and the census key you land on is decided by the ACCIDENT of
the host's wording: 'not a function' collides into not-a-procedure, everything
else ('not iterable', 'cannot read properties') lands in class:TypeError. Two
lessons follow. First, treat any `class:TypeError` or `not-a-procedure` whose
message reads like host internals as a MISSING GUARD in a verb, not a real
language diagnosis. Second, the split between these two keys is not meaningful
about your program — it is meaningful about which JS builtin leaked — so when you
harden a verb, harden it against both wordings at once by checking the Scheme type
up front."

  :census-verbs (
    "(stats/error-count-of \"class:TypeError\")"
    "(stats/error-count-of \"not-a-procedure\")  ;; the colliding sibling"
    "(stats/error-counts 20)")

  :related-cards ("not-a-procedure" "misc-uncategorized" "silent-non-error" "class-ErrorObject"))
